Patric Mueller <bhaak@bigfoot.com> writes:
> robin <usenet@unrated.net> wrote:
> 
> > Any other package, regardless of whether it originates as a fork of
> > NetHack itself, is _not_ NetHack; it's something else.  Common usage
> > in the open source world would certainly allow a forked project to use
> > its parent project's name as _part_ of its own name, but certainly not
> > to claim the original name for its own.
> 
> This is wrong. The NetHack license does not force anybody to change
> the name of the program. Few licenses do this. For example the LaTeX
> Project Public License does but that is more out of the necessity of
> not providing incompatible LaTeX packages.

Robin didn't say the _license_ would require a namechange; he said
common usage would. It is conventional for a fork to take a modified
name (though, if it becomes the main branch, the modification may fall
into desuetude).

The precedents here are mixed: when Andries Brouwer "continuously
deformed" Jay Fenlason's Hack into "an entirely different game" (as
the manpage described it), he kept the same name. However, when Mike
Stephenson "continued the perversion", he adopted the name NetHack at
aeb's request, because the latter "may eventually release a new
version of his own". Since the present DevTeam certainly has the same
intention, it's only reasonable to extend them the same courtesy.

